markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Variables: What They Are, Why We Use Them

This lesson opens with a quick comparison: three social media profiles. Some things stay the same across all of them, the layout, the buttons. Other things change, the name, the photo, the follower count.

That distinction, what stays the same versus what changes, is the whole motivation for variables. Students build a simple chatbot as the vehicle for learning them.

<div style="text-align: center; margin: 20px auto;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/0df910d24411432dfe653654fbde05b6-image-1790795232266.png" alt="Three social media profile cards showing Jada, Simon, and Mira. Each has a different name, photo, and post and follower count, but the same layout and button styling." style="width: 55%; border-radius: 10px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #555555; margin-top: 8px;">Same layout and buttons across all three. Different names, photos, and counts.</div>
</div>

---

### Variables

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128214; Variable:</strong> a container that stores a value in memory.
  </div>
</div>

In the curriculum's example, <code style="font-size:16px;">posts</code> is a name the student chose, and <code style="font-size:16px;">31</code> is the value stored inside it.

<div style="background-color: #1F1976; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8;">
posts = 31
</div>

### Data Types

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128214; Data type:</strong> the format of the data a variable can hold.
  </div>
</div>

Students will use three of these most often:

<ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; font-size: 16px;">
  <li><strong>Integer</strong> (<code style="font-size:16px;">int</code>): a whole number</li><br>
  <li><strong>String</strong> (<code style="font-size:16px;">string</code>): text</li><br>
  <li><strong>Boolean</strong> (<code style="font-size:16px;">bool</code>): True or False</li>
</ul>

<div style="background-color: #1F1976; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8; line-height: 2;">
posts = 31 <span style="color:#ACA8EA;"># int</span><br>
name = "Jada" <span style="color:#ACA8EA;"># string</span><br>
following = True <span style="color:#ACA8EA;"># bool</span>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128161; Teacher tip:</strong> students choose their own variable names. Slide 12's own teaching tip is worth repeating to your class: descriptive names (<code style="font-size:16px;">posts</code>, not <code style="font-size:16px;">x</code>) make code easier for anyone, including their future selves, to read.
  </div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #D5EFFF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-top: 20px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #06338D; line-height: 1.6;">
    <strong>&#8505; Note:</strong> this PL assumes basic familiarity with Python and doesn't re-teach it in depth. If you'd like more practice before teaching this lesson, two good options: work through the curriculum lessons yourself before your students do, and/or complete the Intro to Python Programming PL module.
  </div>
</div>
MARKDOWN
